1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,200
Got a text saying you owe an unpaid toll?

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:05,840
Don't tap the link. Not yet.

3
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:09,140
That text is a scam that keeps coming back,

4
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:12,480
and both the FBI and the Federal Trade Commission have warned

5
00:00:12,430 --> 00:00:13,780
people about it.

6
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:16,300
In the next few minutes, I'll show you the three red

7
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:19,220
flags that give it away, how to check if you really

8
00:00:19,170 --> 00:00:21,320
owe a toll in about thirty seconds,

9
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:25,540
without clicking anything, and exactly what to do if you already

10
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:26,940
tapped the link or paid.

11
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:30,240
Stay to the end, because there's one person in your life

12
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:32,060
who needs to hear this too.

13
00:00:32,528 --> 00:00:33,358
How it works.

14
00:00:33,308 --> 00:00:37,678
The FBI says it started getting complaints about these texts in

15
00:00:37,628 --> 00:00:44,058
March 2024, and the FTC put out its own warning in January 2025.

16
00:00:44,388 --> 00:00:46,118
They haven't gone away.

17
00:00:46,528 --> 00:00:49,758
Here's a composite example, not a real message.

18
00:00:50,108 --> 00:00:52,958
A text arrives from a number you don't know.

19
00:00:53,008 --> 00:00:55,778
It says you have an outstanding toll balance,

20
00:00:55,768 --> 00:00:58,598
usually a small amount, around twelve dollars.

21
00:00:58,548 --> 00:01:01,258
It warns that a late fee is coming,

22
00:01:01,208 --> 00:01:03,938
or worse, if you don't pay today.

23
00:01:04,188 --> 00:01:05,678
And there's a link.

24
00:01:06,068 --> 00:01:09,238
The link looks almost official, because the first part of the

25
00:01:09,188 --> 00:01:12,718
web address copies the name of a real toll service.

26
00:01:12,948 --> 00:01:16,558
The FBI says the sender's phone number often changes from state

27
00:01:16,508 --> 00:01:20,538
to state, so the text seems local wherever you are.

28
00:01:20,688 --> 00:01:23,158
Tap the link, and you land on a page that looks

29
00:01:23,108 --> 00:01:24,898
like a toll payment site.

30
00:01:25,008 --> 00:01:26,938
It asks for your card number.

31
00:01:27,008 --> 00:01:29,918
Sometimes it asks for more, like your name,

32
00:01:29,868 --> 00:01:32,478
your address or your driver's license number.

33
00:01:32,748 --> 00:01:34,558
The small amount is the trick.

34
00:01:34,508 --> 00:01:38,098
Twelve dollars doesn't feel worth arguing about.

35
00:01:38,088 --> 00:01:41,098
But the scammers aren't after the twelve dollars.

36
00:01:41,048 --> 00:01:44,538
They're after the card number and the personal information you

37
00:01:44,488 --> 00:01:45,438
type in.

38
00:01:45,879 --> 00:01:47,689
Three red flags.

39
00:01:48,139 --> 00:01:49,689
Red flag number one:

40
00:01:49,719 --> 00:01:53,169
it came out of nowhere and it wants money now.

41
00:01:53,659 --> 00:01:57,609
The FTC describes these texts exactly that way.

42
00:01:57,559 --> 00:02:00,929
Unexpected, claiming you owe unpaid tolls,

43
00:02:00,879 --> 00:02:03,029
and demanding immediate payment.

44
00:02:03,259 --> 00:02:06,869
The FTC says scammers want you to react quickly.

45
00:02:07,159 --> 00:02:09,769
Real billing problems give you time to check.

46
00:02:10,299 --> 00:02:13,189
Red flag number two: the threat.

47
00:02:13,259 --> 00:02:18,169
A big late fee, a penalty, a deadline measured in hours.

48
00:02:18,459 --> 00:02:20,189
One example the Washington, D.C.

49
00:02:20,139 --> 00:02:23,829
attorney general's office shared gave people just twelve hours

50
00:02:23,779 --> 00:02:27,149
to settle the balance to avoid severe penalties.

51
00:02:27,519 --> 00:02:29,109
Pressure is the tool.

52
00:02:29,179 --> 00:02:32,929
The tighter the deadline, the more careful you should be.

53
00:02:33,139 --> 00:02:35,869
Red flag number three: the link.

54
00:02:36,099 --> 00:02:38,249
Look at the end of the web address.

55
00:02:38,419 --> 00:02:39,029
The D.C.

56
00:02:38,979 --> 00:02:42,749
attorney general's office warns that the first part copies a real

57
00:02:42,699 --> 00:02:45,929
tolling authority, but the ending is unusual.

58
00:02:46,139 --> 00:02:49,949
That same office says toll enforcement agencies will never text

59
00:02:49,899 --> 00:02:52,669
you to demand payment for an unpaid toll.

60
00:02:52,839 --> 00:02:55,509
If the text is pushing you to pay through a link,

61
00:02:55,539 --> 00:02:58,709
treat it as a scam until you've checked on your own.

62
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:01,220
How to check without clicking.

63
00:03:01,170 --> 00:03:03,440
This takes about thirty seconds.

64
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:05,900
Don't use anything from the text.

65
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:08,020
Not the link, not the phone number,

66
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:09,500
and don't reply.

67
00:03:09,710 --> 00:03:13,020
Instead, find your state's toll service yourself.

68
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:15,920
Use the website on your own toll statement,

69
00:03:15,910 --> 00:03:18,780
the website printed on your toll transponder account,

70
00:03:18,730 --> 00:03:22,280
or search for your state's official toll agency and type the

71
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:23,700
address in yourself.

72
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:27,180
Log in to your account there, or call the customer service

73
00:03:27,130 --> 00:03:28,840
number you found on your own.

74
00:03:29,030 --> 00:03:34,080
That's exactly what the FBI and the FTC both tell people to do:

75
00:03:34,110 --> 00:03:36,860
check your account through the legitimate website,

76
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:39,560
or call the real customer service number.

77
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:42,860
If you really do owe a toll, it will be there.

78
00:03:42,930 --> 00:03:45,720
If it isn't, you've just stopped a scam.

79
00:03:45,970 --> 00:03:47,840
Then delete the text.

80
00:03:48,339 --> 00:03:50,849
If you already tapped the link or paid.

81
00:03:51,379 --> 00:03:53,309
First, don't blame yourself.

82
00:03:53,499 --> 00:03:54,029
The D.C.

83
00:03:53,979 --> 00:03:57,689
attorney general's office says these scams are designed to fool

84
00:03:57,639 --> 00:03:58,929
busy people.

85
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:01,349
Tapping a link isn't the end of the world.

86
00:04:01,439 --> 00:04:04,009
What matters is what you typed in.

87
00:04:04,039 --> 00:04:07,129
If you entered a card number, call your bank or card

88
00:04:07,079 --> 00:04:10,829
company right away, using the number on the back of your card.

89
00:04:10,859 --> 00:04:13,729
Tell them it was a scam, ask them to block the

90
00:04:13,679 --> 00:04:16,789
card or send you a new one, and dispute any charges

91
00:04:16,739 --> 00:04:18,269
you don't recognize.

92
00:04:18,659 --> 00:04:22,689
The FTC says federal law protects you from unauthorized use of

93
00:04:22,639 --> 00:04:25,989
your credit card, and there are federal protections for debit cards

94
00:04:25,939 --> 00:04:26,709
too.

95
00:04:26,859 --> 00:04:30,109
If you entered personal information, like your address,

96
00:04:30,059 --> 00:04:32,489
date of birth or driver's license number,

97
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:35,189
go to IdentityTheft.gov.

98
00:04:35,139 --> 00:04:38,009
It walks you through a personal recovery plan.

99
00:04:38,039 --> 00:04:42,009
You can also consider a credit freeze with the three credit bureaus.

100
00:04:41,979 --> 00:04:44,349
The FTC says a freeze is free,

101
00:04:44,299 --> 00:04:46,849
and it makes it harder for someone to open a new

102
00:04:46,799 --> 00:04:48,409
account in your name.

103
00:04:48,579 --> 00:04:50,549
And watch for a second wave.

104
00:04:50,619 --> 00:04:53,809
Be very skeptical of any follow-up call,

105
00:04:53,779 --> 00:04:56,509
text or email that offers to help you,

106
00:04:56,459 --> 00:04:59,709
asks for payment, or asks for more information.

107
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:01,857
Report it.

108
00:05:02,127 --> 00:05:04,777
Reporting takes a minute and it helps.

109
00:05:05,087 --> 00:05:08,357
On most phones you can use the report junk option right

110
00:05:08,307 --> 00:05:11,802
in your messaging app, or forward the text to seven,

111
00:05:11,752 --> 00:05:13,317
seven, two, six.

112
00:05:13,267 --> 00:05:14,977
That spells SPAM.

113
00:05:15,147 --> 00:05:19,357
That lets your carrier review it and possibly block similar messages.

114
00:05:19,547 --> 00:05:25,597
You can also report it to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov,

115
00:05:25,547 --> 00:05:31,417
and to the FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center at IC3.gov.

116
00:05:31,367 --> 00:05:34,237
The FBI asks you to include the phone number the text

117
00:05:34,187 --> 00:05:37,117
came from and the web address in the text.

118
00:05:37,347 --> 00:05:41,357
Then tell your family, especially anyone who drives a lot or

119
00:05:41,307 --> 00:05:43,017
pays tolls by mail.

120
00:05:43,087 --> 00:05:44,357
Not as a lecture.

121
00:05:44,327 --> 00:05:46,357
Just send them this one rule:

122
00:05:46,467 --> 00:05:50,117
if a text says you owe money, don't use its link.

123
00:05:50,207 --> 00:05:52,377
Go to the real website yourself.

124
00:05:52,567 --> 00:05:55,177
That one habit stops this scam,

125
00:05:55,127 --> 00:05:58,357
and a lot of others like it, from fake delivery texts

126
00:05:58,307 --> 00:06:00,057
to fake bank alerts.

127
00:06:00,726 --> 00:06:02,416
So, one more time.

128
00:06:02,686 --> 00:06:06,996
Unexpected, urgent, and a strange link means stop.

129
00:06:07,166 --> 00:06:10,436
Check on your own, report it, delete it.

130
00:06:10,406 --> 00:06:13,356
And if you already clicked, call your bank and go to

131
00:06:13,306 --> 00:06:15,536
IdentityTheft.gov.

132
00:06:15,486 --> 00:06:19,076
This channel gives general information, not legal or financial

133
00:06:19,026 --> 00:06:21,596
advice, and the official links are in the description.

134
00:06:21,546 --> 00:06:25,836
Next time, we'll look at the wrong-number text that turns into

135
00:06:25,786 --> 00:06:27,176
a long con.

